---
import IllustrationFigure from "~/components/illustration-kit/IllustrationFigure.astro"
import SvgFigure from "~/components/illustration-kit/SvgFigure.astro"
import { series, stroke } from "~/data/palette"
---

<IllustrationFigure
  name="repugnant-conclusion"
  lead="Each step can look acceptable. At the end, the population is enormous and lives are barely worthwhile."
>
  <SvgFigure
    name="repugnant-populations"
    class="mx-auto w-full max-w-lg"
    viewBox="0 0 420 254"
    title="Parfit's sequence from population A through A plus and B to Z"
    desc="The width of each bar represents how many people there are, and the height how good their lives are. A has a small population with very high wellbeing. A plus adds more people with less good but worthwhile lives without changing the original people's wellbeing. B has the same population as A plus, with everyone at a common level above A plus's average. Repeating similar comparisons leads toward Z, a vastly larger population with lives barely worth living. Widths are not to scale."
  >
    <g fill="currentColor" font-size="19" font-weight="700">
      <text x="20" y="24">
        A
      </text>
      <text x="104" y="24">
        A+
      </text>
      <text x="195" y="24">
        B
      </text>
      <text x="258" y="24">
        Z
      </text>
    </g>
    <path d="M12 222 H408" stroke={stroke} stroke-width="1.5" />
    <rect x="20" y="79" width="16" height="143" fill={series.blue} fill-opacity=".7" />
    <rect x="104" y="79" width="16" height="143" fill={series.blue} fill-opacity=".7" />
    <rect x="120" y="137" width="16" height="85" fill={series.gold} fill-opacity=".7" />
    <rect x="195" y="100" width="32" height="122" fill={series.blue} fill-opacity=".7" />
    <rect x="258" y="194" width="150" height="28" fill={series.lilac} fill-opacity=".7" />
    <g fill="none" stroke={series.red} stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round">
      <path d="M53 150 H96 m-6 -6 6 6 -6 6" />
      <path d="M151 150 H187 m-6 -6 6 6 -6 6" />
      <path d="M236 150 H251 m-6 -6 6 6 -6 6" />
    </g>
    <g fill="currentColor" font-size="17">
      <text x="28" y="245" text-anchor="middle">
        Few
      </text>
      <text x="120" y="245" text-anchor="middle">
        More
      </text>
      <text x="211" y="245" text-anchor="middle">
        As many
      </text>
      <text x="333" y="245" text-anchor="middle">
        Vastly more
      </text>
      <text x="20" y="61">
        High wellbeing
      </text>
      <text x="244" y="135" text-anchor="middle">
        …
      </text>
      <text x="409" y="188" text-anchor="end">
        Barely positive
      </text>
    </g>
  </SvgFigure>
  <p class="text-base">
    <strong>Width</strong> shows population size; <strong>height</strong> shows wellbeing per
    person. The total shaded area grows at each step, although average wellbeing is much lower in Z than in A.
    Schematic, not to scale.
  </p>
  <Fragment slot="caption">
    A+ adds worthwhile lives to A. B has the same number of people as A+, all above A+'s average
    wellbeing. Repeat these apparently acceptable steps and the argument reaches Z. Whether
    those comparisons really make Z better than A is the dispute.
  </Fragment>
</IllustrationFigure>
